1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,973
Here's a trap specific to this kind of work.

2
00:00:02,973 --> 00:00:07,980
Price by the hour, or by the individual asset, and you've just built an incentive to

3
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:12,510
generate more and test less, exactly backwards from Section 9.2's economics.

4
00:00:12,510 --> 00:00:13,702
Generation is cheap.

5
00:00:13,702 --> 00:00:18,590
The value is in the discipline around it, and hourly pricing charges for the wrong

6
00:00:18,590 --> 00:00:19,603
half of the work.

7
00:00:19,603 --> 00:00:24,610
Price the sprint itself instead, the whole five-step rhythm from Section 9.1, as one

8
00:00:24,610 --> 00:00:24,908
unit.

9
00:00:24,908 --> 00:00:29,856
That aligns what you're paid for with what actually matters: running the discipline

10
00:00:29,856 --> 00:00:33,670
every week, not producing the largest possible pile of creative.

11
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:35,995
This isn't about charging more or less.

12
00:00:35,995 --> 00:00:40,644
It's about charging for the right thing, so the incentive built into the price

13
00:00:40,644 --> 00:00:45,592
agrees with the incentive built into the system, instead of quietly working against

14
00:00:45,592 --> 00:00:45,771
it.

15
00:00:45,771 --> 00:00:50,718
Ask yourself what a prospective client is actually paying for, honestly, before you

16
00:00:50,718 --> 00:00:52,089
decide how to price it.

17
00:00:52,089 --> 00:00:57,096
If the honest answer is "the weekly discipline that finds a winner and protects it,"

18
00:00:57,096 --> 00:00:58,288
price that directly.

19
00:00:58,288 --> 00:01:02,759
If the honest answer is still "a pile of video files," you haven't finished

20
00:01:02,759 --> 00:01:07,706
explaining the system yet, and that's worth fixing before the pricing conversation,

21
00:01:07,706 --> 00:01:08,541
not during it.

22
00:01:09,091 --> 00:01:13,867
The retainer row is the closest match to what Section 9.1 actually built.

23
00:01:13,867 --> 00:01:19,298
You're not selling creative pieces, you're selling the weekly rhythm that finds and

24
00:01:19,298 --> 00:01:24,795
protects a winner, and a retainer prices exactly that, regardless of whether a given

25
00:01:24,795 --> 00:01:28,263
week produces one dramatic result or five quiet ones.

26
00:01:28,263 --> 00:01:32,385
The third row deserves a caution the table doesn't fully carry.

27
00:01:32,385 --> 00:01:37,751
Pricing against media spend only makes sense once Section 7's testing architecture

28
00:01:37,751 --> 00:01:42,004
is actually in place and the CAC ceiling is real, not guessed at.

29
00:01:42,004 --> 00:01:47,500
Offering that structure before the testing architecture exists ties your income to a

30
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:50,707
number nobody's actually earned control over yet.

31
00:01:50,707 --> 00:01:56,072
Whichever model you choose, resist blending it with per-asset pricing on the side.

32
00:01:56,072 --> 00:02:01,569
A retainer with an hourly add-on for "extra" creative quietly reintroduces the exact

33
00:02:01,569 --> 00:02:05,037
incentive problem the retainer was supposed to solve.

34
00:02:05,037 --> 00:02:09,617
This is also where Section 9.3's ownership question comes back around.

35
00:02:09,617 --> 00:02:14,852
A solo operator pricing a retainer is pricing their own fifteen hours, honestly,

36
00:02:14,852 --> 00:02:17,600
against what those hours actually protect.

37
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:22,966
An agency pricing the same retainer across a pod of people needs the same honesty,

38
00:02:22,966 --> 00:02:25,125
just spread across more salaries.

39
00:02:25,125 --> 00:02:26,237
The unit changes.

40
00:02:26,237 --> 00:02:31,014
The underlying question, what is this actually worth to protect, doesn't.

41
00:02:31,564 --> 00:02:36,519
Two components, and notice the second only activates once a winner is real, not

42
00:02:36,519 --> 00:02:37,146
hoped for.

43
00:02:37,146 --> 00:02:38,651
That sequencing matters.

44
00:02:38,651 --> 00:02:43,732
Pricing for scale before anything has actually scaled is pricing against a result

45
00:02:43,732 --> 00:02:48,874
you can't yet promise, the same mistake Section 10.1 already warned against making

46
00:02:48,874 --> 00:02:50,129
in the pitch itself.

47
00:02:50,129 --> 00:02:55,397
Agree on the scale trigger's terms before you need them, in writing, during the same

48
00:02:55,397 --> 00:02:57,655
conversation that sets the retainer.

49
00:02:57,655 --> 00:03:02,422
Negotiating a price increase in the middle of a winning streak is an awkward

50
00:03:02,422 --> 00:03:04,617
conversation for everyone involved.

51
00:03:04,617 --> 00:03:09,823
Agreeing to the terms while nothing dramatic is happening yet is a much easier one.

52
00:03:09,823 --> 00:03:12,081
Why does the order matter this much?

53
00:03:12,081 --> 00:03:17,286
Because a scale trigger negotiated after a winner already exists puts both sides in

54
00:03:17,286 --> 00:03:18,227
an unfair spot.

55
00:03:18,227 --> 00:03:22,994
The client feels pressured to accept a higher number to keep momentum going.

56
00:03:22,994 --> 00:03:28,199
You feel pressured to accept whatever they'll agree to, rather than what the larger

57
00:03:28,199 --> 00:03:30,959
media budget genuinely justifies protecting.

58
00:03:30,959 --> 00:03:36,102
Deciding the terms before either pressure exists protects the relationship as much

59
00:03:36,102 --> 00:03:36,917
as the price.

60
00:03:37,467 --> 00:03:41,331
Two requests are worth refusing outright, not negotiating down.

61
00:03:41,331 --> 00:03:46,422
A guaranteed result asks you to promise something Section 8's entire tree exists to

62
00:03:46,422 --> 00:03:48,507
prove nobody can promise honestly.

63
00:03:48,507 --> 00:03:53,475
Taking that deal means either lying to win the client, or quietly padding results

64
00:03:53,475 --> 00:03:57,462
later to look like you kept a promise you never should have made.

65
00:03:57,462 --> 00:04:02,613
Skipping pre-flight to launch faster asks you to remove the exact check that catches

66
00:04:02,613 --> 00:04:06,723
an overreaching claim or a licence gap before it spends real money.

67
00:04:06,723 --> 00:04:11,813
Section 6 built that gate for a reason that doesn't go away just because a client's

68
00:04:11,813 --> 00:04:12,488
in a hurry.

69
00:04:12,488 --> 00:04:17,149
Refusing this one is also the easier of the two conversations: it's not your

70
00:04:17,149 --> 00:04:22,240
judgement being overruled, it's the platform's own compliance risk being ignored on

71
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:23,466
the client's behalf.

72
00:04:23,466 --> 00:04:28,189
Both refusals get easier with practice, and both get harder to hold if you've

73
00:04:28,189 --> 00:04:32,053
already accepted the client's money before the request arrives.

74
00:04:32,053 --> 00:04:37,205
Name these two boundaries in the same conversation as the pricing itself, before any

75
00:04:37,205 --> 00:04:41,498
retainer is signed, so refusing later isn't a surprise to either side.

76
00:04:42,048 --> 00:04:46,866
Next lecture builds the portfolio that makes this pricing conversation credible in

77
00:04:46,866 --> 00:04:51,742
the first place, using exactly the reports Section 9.5 already taught you to write.

78
00:04:51,742 --> 00:04:56,559
Before that, write down your own version of the two refusals from this lecture, in

79
00:04:56,559 --> 00:04:57,441
your own words.

80
00:04:57,441 --> 00:05:02,376
Having them ready before a client asks is the difference between refusing calmly and

81
00:05:02,376 --> 00:05:06,723
getting talked out of a boundary you never actually decided on in advance.

82
00:05:06,723 --> 00:05:09,661
Would either refusal actually cost you the client?

83
00:05:09,661 --> 00:05:12,246
Maybe, on a bad day with the wrong prospect.

84
00:05:12,246 --> 00:05:17,004
Weigh that honestly against what saying yes would cost you instead: a promise you

85
00:05:17,004 --> 00:05:21,293
can't keep, or your name attached to a claim Section 6 would have caught.

86
00:05:21,293 --> 00:05:26,228
Most clients worth keeping respect the boundary once you explain it clearly, and the

87
00:05:26,228 --> 00:05:30,341
ones who don't were never going to be an easy relationship regardless.
